WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.960
Jealousy, ego, and not knowing what a wolverine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:07.937
is — these actors turned down movie&nbsp;
parts for some peculiar reasons.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:14.340
In November of 2021, "Fast &amp; Furious" veteran&nbsp;
Vin Diesel took to Instagram to plead with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:18.420
Dwayne Johnson to participate in the 10th&nbsp;
installment of the long-running franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:24.180
"Fast X." Johnson had previously stated that he&nbsp;
would not be returning, which many believe is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:28.260
due to a feud between the actors that has&nbsp;
spanned more than six years and counting.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:32.760
However, Diesel still tried to seemingly&nbsp;
guilt trip the actor in front of his 82&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:37.260
million followers on social media, claiming&nbsp;
that his children still refer to Johnson as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:42.300
"Uncle Dwayne" and that Johnson's character of&nbsp;
Luke Hobbs could never be recast or replaced.

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:47.700
Not long afterward, Johnson sat down with CNN&nbsp;
and set the record straight. "The Rock" told&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:51.780
the world that he and Diesel had come to&nbsp;
an agreement in private. Long story short:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:55.000
the former wrestler would not&nbsp;
be returning to the franchise.

00:00:55.273 --> 00:00:59.675
"Your mistake is thinking you've&nbsp;
got a goddamn choice, boy."

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:04.380
Johnson said that he was "very surprised"&nbsp;
by Diesel's Instagram post, expressing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:08.250
his disappointment in the actor's&nbsp;
clear "manipulation." He continued —

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:10.440
"I didn't like that he brought&nbsp;
up his children in the post,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:13.200
as well as Paul Walker's&nbsp;
death. Leave them out of it."

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:18.060
It's safe to say that Diesel's impassioned request&nbsp;
didn't make much of a difference in Johnson's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:23.340
decision. Despite his frustration at the very&nbsp;
public way that it was handled, Johnson maintains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:28.431
that he will always cheer for the success of&nbsp;
the "Fast &amp; Furious" movies and their cast.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:32.100
"A League of Their Own" is&nbsp;
a fictionalized portrayal of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:35.280
the All-American Girls Professional&nbsp;
Baseball League and its best team,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:40.080
the Rockford Peaches. Debra Winger was&nbsp;
originally cast in the lead role, and she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:45.240
spent months training with the Chicago Cubs in&nbsp;
order to increase her authenticity. However, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:49.980
moment that Madonna was added to the project, the&nbsp;
"Terms of Endearment" actress was out the door.

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:53.640
Winger believed that, rather than&nbsp;
"A League of Their Own" being the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:57.480
historically empowering story it was meant&nbsp;
to be, the Queen of Pop's involvement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:01.290
would drive the film in a different&nbsp;
direction. She told The Telegraph —

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:03.660
"I think [her] acting career&nbsp;
has spoken for itself."

00:02:04.035 --> 00:02:06.075
"And what am I supposed to do, huh?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:12.060
Go back to taxi dancing? Ten cents so&nbsp;
some slob can sweat gin all over me?"

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:15.000
Despite the film's eventual positive reception,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:19.140
the actress had a few choice words to&nbsp;
say about the final product — like her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:23.370
claim that Madonna lacked the proper&nbsp;
preparation. Winger told Vanity Fair —

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:27.600
"As entertaining as [the final film]&nbsp;
was, you don't walk away going, 'Wow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:31.191
those women did that.' You&nbsp;
kind of go, 'Is that true?'"

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:37.980
In 2013, production for the Natalie Portman&nbsp;
Western film "Jane Got a Gun" was about to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:42.960
kick off when it hit a major snag. After a&nbsp;
three-day standoff over delays and control of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:47.880
the final cut with producer Scott Steindorff,&nbsp;
director Lynne Ramsay stepped away from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:52.440
project the day that production was supposed&nbsp;
to begin, leaving a chaotic environment behind.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:57.240
Ramsay's choice to quit the film ultimately&nbsp;
led to the departure of cinematographer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:02.340
Darius Khondji and actor Jude Law, who was&nbsp;
originally set to play the movie's antagonist.

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:07.560
Several unnamed sources claim that Law had&nbsp;
admitted to only signing up for the project for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:13.260
the opportunity to work alongside Ramsay, although&nbsp;
he hasn't confirmed this himself. Regardless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:17.640
without her in the director's chair, he seemingly&nbsp;
had no interest in continuing with the project.

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:23.160
Law was later replaced by Bradley Cooper, who&nbsp;
also ended up dropping out due to scheduling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:26.820
conflicts. The movie would finally&nbsp;
hit theaters with Ewan McGregor in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:31.320
the villainous role and Gavin O'Connor in&nbsp;
the director's chair. Partially as a result&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:36.360
of the cast and crew controversy, "Jane&nbsp;
Got a Gun" was a box office flop. Today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:40.855
it's primarily remembered as a low point&nbsp;
for the production company that financed it.

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:45.480
Steve McQueen spent the '60s and&nbsp;
'70s enjoying the fruits of stardom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:48.720
appearing as the leading man in&nbsp;
countless films that would go on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:53.640
to become classics. Over three decades after&nbsp;
his death, McQueen still holds the moniker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:57.540
of the "king of cool" thanks to his long&nbsp;
list of interesting hobbies and talents.

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:01.560
During casting for the Western classic&nbsp;
"Butch Cassidy and the Sundance Kid,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:05.700
McQueen accepted the role of Sundance&nbsp;
after reading through the script. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:10.020
he wound up stepping away from the role over&nbsp;
disagreements with his co-star, Paul Newman.

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:14.820
McQueen's undeniable star power led him&nbsp;
to refuse second billing to another actor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:19.380
despite the fact that Newman was arguably&nbsp;
more famous at the time. The role later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:23.520
went to Robert Redford, in what has become&nbsp;
one of his career defining performances.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:27.600
"Butch Cassidy and the Sundance Kid"&nbsp;
went on to garner widespread acclaim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:33.291
soaring to the top of the charts as the biggest&nbsp;
box office draw for two weeks after its release.

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:40.620
In 2019, Charlize Theron sat down with Andy Cohen&nbsp;
on "Watch What Happens Live" and discussed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:45.420
topic of ageism in Hollywood. When Theron was&nbsp;
asked by a fan why she reportedly turned down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:49.740
the monumental role of Wonder Woman, the "Mad&nbsp;
Max: Fury Road" star set the record straight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:54.480
by sharing the real story. Theron explained&nbsp;
that she was discussing possible involvement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:59.040
in the film when someone informed her that she&nbsp;
would actually be taking on a supporting role.

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:01.860
"I'm not familiar with it, I&nbsp;
don't really know, like — I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:06.975
what does Wonder Woman do? And my — this person&nbsp;
said, 'No, it's for Wonder Woman's mom.'"

00:05:06.975 --> 00:05:10.800
Theron naturally turned the role down,&nbsp;
and seems to be able to laugh about it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:14.340
now. She called it a "defining&nbsp;
moment" in her Hollywood career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:19.080
in which she had crossed over to a completely&nbsp;
new category of roles without even realizing it.

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:25.440
You might remember Ja Rule from his&nbsp;
hip-hop heyday in the 2000s — or maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:29.580
you know him as the co-founder of the&nbsp;
disastrous Fyre Festival. Regardless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:34.740
in the summer of 2002, Ja Rule was riding&nbsp;
a career high — he was one of the biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:38.760
rappers on the planet and secured that&nbsp;
year's Billboard Award for top rap album.

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:43.680
Ja Rule had a minor part in "The Fast and the&nbsp;
Furious," the first film in what would become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:48.780
a massive franchise. He was later approached to&nbsp;
be upgraded to a starring role for the sequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:52.560
"2 Fast 2 Furious," along with&nbsp;
a cozy half-a-million-dollar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:56.580
payday. Surprisingly the rapper was&nbsp;
reluctant to participate in the film.

00:05:56.580 --> 00:06:03.240
Rule allegedly acted like a bit of a prima&nbsp;
donna during the casting process. In 2015, "2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:09.038
Fast" director John Singleton explained that the&nbsp;
rapper's ego had become inflated. He elaborated —

00:06:09.137 --> 00:06:13.140
"He wouldn't return calls. I went to the&nbsp;
studio to go see him [...] He was kinda&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:16.680
playing me to the side and I was&nbsp;
like, 'What? What is this s---?'"

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:21.480
When talking to MTV about the ordeal,&nbsp;
Rule pointed to his desire to make a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:24.240
more serious name for himself&nbsp;
in the acting world, saying —

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:29.195
"I just felt it wasn't the best move for me as&nbsp;
far as what I want to do in Hollywood right now."

00:06:29.195 --> 00:06:31.620
The frustrated director eventually reached out to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:36.807
Ludacris to create a new role in place&nbsp;
of Rule's, and the rest is history.

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:41.100
"Brokeback Mountain" — the Western&nbsp;
romantic drama starring Heath Ledger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:45.060
and Jake Gyllenhaal — is a beautiful&nbsp;
movie, and it's tough to imagine any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:50.280
other actors portraying the cowboy lovers at&nbsp;
its center. However, if you ask Mark Wahlberg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:54.960
he'll tell you that he was considered for one of&nbsp;
the starring roles, but wound up turning it down.

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:59.880
In a 2007 interview, Wahlberg revealed&nbsp;
his reasoning for declining the role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:01.650
telling Premiere Magazine —

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:06.960
"I read 15 pages of the script and got a little&nbsp;
creeped out. It was very graphic, descriptive —&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:11.495
the spitting on the hand, getting ready to&nbsp;
do the thing [...] it's just not my deal."

00:07:11.495 --> 00:07:15.960
Wahlberg may not be the most reliable&nbsp;
narrator — after all, he's also claimed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.960 --> 00:07:20.280
that he would've single-handedly stopped&nbsp;
9/11. Perhaps he's telling the truth about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:25.440
having had the opportunity to star in Ang&nbsp;
Lee's 2005 classic, but it wouldn't be the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:29.460
first time Wahlberg has exaggerated a little&nbsp;
bit during a conversation with the media.

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:33.660
Many people point to Marky Mark's strict&nbsp;
Catholic beliefs as a possible reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:37.440
for his discomfort in playing&nbsp;
a man attracted to other men.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:41.580
Wahlberg has claimed that being a Catholic&nbsp;
is the "most important aspect" of his life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:48.000
and his recent pivot to faith-based media like&nbsp;
2022's "Father Stu" is further evidence of that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:53.760
The National Enquirer even once reported that&nbsp;
Wahlberg's priest, James Flavin, always has the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:58.650
final say in whether or not the actor should&nbsp;
accept a role. An unnamed insider shared —

00:07:58.650 --> 00:08:01.740
"[Wahlberg] never makes a final&nbsp;
decision on a starring role until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:06.300
Father Flavin gives his OK. Mark says&nbsp;
he owes his career to Father Flavin."

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:09.420
It seems that Wahlberg may&nbsp;
not have made the right call,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.420 --> 00:08:14.100
as far as money is concerned. The legendary&nbsp;
performances of Ledger and Gyllenhaal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.100 --> 00:08:20.368
earned "Brokeback Mountain" three Academy&nbsp;
Awards and $178 million at the box office.

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:26.400
It's hard to imagine anyone other than Hugh&nbsp;
Jackman playing the mutant superhero Wolverine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:31.020
but Russell Crowe almost took the role for&nbsp;
himself. Crowe was the first choice to play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.020 --> 00:08:36.000
Logan in 2000's "X-Men," but the legendary&nbsp;
actor turned down the role over fears of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:41.880
being typecast. What type was Crowe worried about&nbsp;
being cast as? Why, a wolf enthusiast, of course.

00:08:41.880 --> 00:08:47.640
Given that Crowe's iconic character of Maximus in&nbsp;
the film "Gladiator" is associated with wolves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:52.200
the actor was concerned about becoming&nbsp;
Hollywood's go-to "Mr. Wolf," according&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:58.080
to News.com.au. He didn't seem to know or&nbsp;
care that wolverines aren't wolves at all.

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:02.940
Given the ongoing multi-billion-dollar franchise&nbsp;
that was born from the first "X-Men" film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:07.800
Crowe losing out on the role of a lifetime&nbsp;
seems like a huge misstep that could have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:13.860
led to years of regret. But in a 2019 interview&nbsp;
on "The Howard Stern Show," Crowe expressed his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.860 --> 00:09:17.638
respect for Jackman's performance and&nbsp;
showed that he had no hard feelings.

00:09:17.777 --> 00:09:25.577
"I wouldn't have carried it through with the&nbsp;
grace and the, the direction that Hugh gave it."

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:30.060
In fact, it was Crowe who recommended&nbsp;
Jackman — a relatively unknown actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:32.040
at the time — to replace him in the role.

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:37.620
Will Smith has appeared in his fair share of&nbsp;
headlines over the years. Whether it's the Oscar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:43.080
slap heard 'round the world or his complicated&nbsp;
relationship with his wife Jada, the actor is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:48.540
pretty familiar with being in the spotlight. Back&nbsp;
when prepping began for Quentin Tarantino's 2012&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.540 --> 00:09:54.240
film "Django Unchained," Will Smith was offered&nbsp;
the opportunity to play the titular character of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.240 --> 00:10:00.840
Django, which would eventually go to Jamie Foxx.&nbsp;
In 2013, Smith sat down with Entertainment Weekly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.840 --> 00:10:05.700
and explained his choice to turn down the&nbsp;
role. His explanation was short and sweet:

00:10:06.035 --> 00:10:07.775
"Django wasn't the lead."

00:10:07.775 --> 00:10:12.000
In Smith's eyes, King Schultz, the&nbsp;
dentist-turned-bounty-hunter played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:17.460
by Christoph Waltz, is the true lead of the film&nbsp;
— after all, Schultz gets to kill the bad guy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.460 --> 00:10:23.040
However, in a much later 2016 interview with&nbsp;
The Hollywood Reporter, Smith claimed that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:27.450
turned down the role due to the overwhelming&nbsp;
use of violence in the story. He explained —

00:10:27.450 --> 00:10:29.640
"I wanted to make the greatest love story that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.640 --> 00:10:32.280
African Americans had ever&nbsp;
seen from American cinema."

00:10:32.280 --> 00:10:37.500
Essentially, Smith didn't want to have to rely&nbsp;
on violence as the answer to racial conflict.

